# Worktree Mechanics

Read this before your first write to any `~/projects` repo, and whenever you start a session inside a worktree. The roles themselves (WORKER vs OWNER) are defined in GLOBAL_RULES.md; this doc is the how.

## The invariant

Each work item gets one worktree under `~/worktrees/{repo}/`, shared by every agent working that item, never one worktree per agent. (Run-scoped builder worktrees inside an item follow the governing work playbook.) The primary checkout at `~/projects/{repo}` is never yours to clean by hand.

## Primary checkout rules

Inside your own worktree, normal git applies. In a primary checkout, mutating tracked content or git history and refs stays worktree-only; sync main via `wt-sync.sh {repo}`. Routine operational work is self-serve right there: reads, `python -m venv` and `pip install`, `crontab` (but not `crontab -r`), `nohup` background runs, writing logs and new, untracked, or gitignored files, and deleting already-merged branches. The guard allows these; do them yourself instead of handing them to the user.

If the primary is dirty and every dirty file is an edited tracked file inside the repo's declared `[restorable]` lanes (`.wt-lanes`), run `primary-restore.sh <repo-root>`: it banks the discarded versions in the agent trash, then resets the files, and it refuses to touch anything outside that shape. For any other dirt in the primary, report the file list and stop.

## Worker contract

You were dispatched into this item's worktree, and {project_root} is that worktree. Read and edit this repo only through it; `~/projects/` paths are for other repos, because the primary's copy of any file this item touches is stale. Commit by pathspec on the work branch. Done means commit plus report, then stop. wt-new, wt-merge, and wt-janitor belong to your dispatcher; never run them.

## Owner mechanics

- Before your first write, run `wt-new.sh {repo} --next <slug>`; from inside a worktree, prefix `WT_NESTED_OK=1`. Workers never do this. For a brand-new project with no repo yet, `repo-new.sh <name>` bootstraps `~/projects/<name>` first.
- When resuming an item, step 0 is `ls ~/worktrees/{repo}/`.
- When iterating against a live server before the merge, run it from the worktree.
- Done includes the merge: commit, then `cd ~/projects/{repo} && wt-merge.sh <worktree>`. "Push" means wt-merge. On any nonzero exit the script prints its own recovery; follow it, and report to the user only when it says to.
- An item you are walking away from ends with `cd ~/projects/{repo} && wt-abandon.sh <worktree> --reason "<why>"` instead: work is preserved recoverably (whole tree in trash 7 days, commits on a recovered ref 14 days), the tree and branch go away, and the item's ledger gates are cancelled. Owner-only, human-invoked, never automated.
- On a merge conflict, resolve only your own hunks; if a stranger's file conflicts, stop and ask. For the hot files (context/INDEX.md, context/WORK_INDEX.md, CODEMAP.md), take main's hunk and re-add only your rows.
- Only `wt-new.sh` creates item worktrees, and only `wt-run.sh` creates or removes manual-mode run worktrees inside one. Never run raw `git worktree add`, and never use the harness EnterWorktree or isolation options on ~/projects repos. The supervisor daemon manages its own run checkouts.
- Leftover run trees (`.runs/`, `work/*/checkouts/`) are reaped only by `wt-reap.sh`, which `wt-merge` calls at item wrap and `wt-janitor` calls fleet-wide. Never delete or trash them by hand.
- `JANITOR:` rows are OWNER work; workers never act on them. Relay `JANITOR:` rows to the user verbatim.

Lane details, merge exit codes, and debris recovery live in `~/projects/fleet/context/entities/worktree-git-durability.md`; every wt script also prints its exact recovery on refusal.

## Sparse worktrees

A repo may commit `.wt-sparse` at its root: one repo-relative directory per
line (`#` comments allowed), each excluded from every new worktree's checkout.
`wt-new.sh` applies it automatically, printing the excluded directories and the
restore command to stderr. Need the excluded files? Run the printed
`git -C <worktree> sparse-checkout disable`. Run trees mirror the item
worktree's sparse state at fork time; after changing the item's shape, re-fork
or run the same disable in existing run trees.

## Enforcement

`bash-guard.sh`, a PreToolUse hook, enforces all of this once `~/.config/agent-worktrees/deny.on` exists. In a primary checkout it is default-allow: it blocks only a short list of operations that lose or corrupt shared state and lets everything else through. Deny messages lead with the self-serve fix; do that fix, and treat asking the user to run a `!` command as the last resort. Never suggest disabling the guard (`deny.on`). Unresolved write targets fail closed; use literal paths. The full row list lives in `~/projects/fleet/context/entities/claude-hooks.md`.
